### PHP Interface Declarations

Source: https://github.com/tree-sitter/tree-sitter-php/blob/master/test/corpus/declarations.txt

Demonstrates the declaration of a PHP interface and a class implementing it. The code shows basic interface definition and method implementation.

```php
<?php

interface ThrowableInterface {
    public function getMessage();
}

class Exception_foo implements ThrowableInterface {
    public $foo = "foo";

    public function getMessage() {
        return $this->foo;
    }
}
```

--------------------------------

### PHP Namespace Use Declarations

Source: https://github.com/tree-sitter/tree-sitter-php/blob/master/test/corpus/declarations.txt

Provides examples of PHP namespace usage, including single imports, aliased imports, grouped imports, and importing functions and constants.

```php
<?php

namespace Foo\Bar\Baz;

use Foo\Bar\Baz;
use Foo\Bar\Baz as Qux;
use Foo\Bar\Baz, Foo\Bar\Qux;

use function Foo\Bar\foo;
use function Foo\Bar\foo as bar;

use const Foo\Bar\BAZ;
use const Foo\Bar\BAZ as QUX;

use Foo\Bar\{Baz, Qux};
use function Foo\Bar\{foo, bar};
use const Foo\Bar\{BAZ, QUX};

use Foo\Bar\{
    Baz,
    Qux as Quux,
    function foo,
    function bar as baz,
    const BAZ,
    const QUX as QUUX
};
```

--------------------------------

### PHP Trait and Use Declarations

Source: https://github.com/tree-sitter/tree-sitter-php/blob/master/test/corpus/declarations.txt

Illustrates the usage of traits in PHP, including multiple trait imports and aliasing methods. It shows how traits can be used within classes and traits themselves.

```php
<?php

trait AbstractTrait
{
    use LoggerAwareTrait;
    use LoggerAwareTrait, OtherTrait {}
    use LoggerAwareTrait, OtherTrait;
}

class AbstractCache
{
    use AbstractTrait {
        deleteItems as private;
        AbstractTrait::deleteItem as delete;
        AbstractTrait::hasItem as has;
    }
}
```

--------------------------------

### PHP First Class Callable Syntax

Source: https://github.com/tree-sitter/tree-sitter-php/blob/master/test/corpus/expressions.txt

Illustrates the first-class callable syntax in PHP, specifically the variadic placeholder (...). This feature allows passing arguments to functions, methods, or object instantiations using the spread operator. The AST captures these calls.

```php
foo(...);
$this->foo(...);
A::foo(...);

// These are invalid, but accepted on the parser level.
new Foo(...);

#[Foo(...)]
function foo() {}
```

```APIDOC
First Class Callable Syntax (Variadic Placeholder):
  - Uses the '...' operator to pass arguments as an array.
  - Applicable to function calls, method calls, and object creation.
  - AST Representation:
    (function_call_expression ...)
    (member_call_expression ...)
    (scoped_call_expression ...)
    (object_creation_expression ...)
```

